
# Development values. These carry no secrets and are safe in version control —
# they exist so that a fresh clone boots with one command. Production supplies
# its own.
#
# This file is also the documentation. Every key the CMS reads is listed here,
# including the ones only production sets, because a key that exists and is not
# written down is a key nobody knows to set.
#
# `scripts/ensure-local-env.js` copies this to `apps/cms/.env` on a fresh clone
# and never overwrites an existing one.
#
# **Production does not use `.env`.** Strapi calls
# `dotenv.config( { path: process.env.ENV_PATH } )`, and the production PM2
# environment sets `ENV_PATH=.env.production` — see
# infra/production/cms-host/pm2/ecosystem.config.cjs. That keeps the
# development file, which this one generates, from being the production file.
#
# Generating a value for anything below marked "random":
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Any length works. Strapi hashes these before use, so there is no format to
# match — only entropy to supply. Never reuse one across two keys, and never
# reuse the development literals here.


# ─── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The interface Strapi binds to.
#
# 0.0.0.0 in development, so a phone on the same network can reach it.
# Production sets 127.0.0.1: nginx is the only client, and binding wider puts
# Strapi on the instance's private address where anything else in the security
# group reaches it directly — bypassing every header nginx sets, and the
# `Host` matching along with it.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# The port Strapi listens on.
#
# In production this must equal CMS_PORT in infra/production/cms-host/.env,
# which is what nginx is told to proxy to. Nothing reconciles the two.
PORT=1337

# How many proxies stand in front of this server.
#
# **No environment default: zero everywhere unless a deployment says so.** Both
# mistakes are bad and they are not symmetric — trusting a header nobody set
# lets any caller claim any address, while not trusting one that is set makes
# every caller share the proxy's address. The first is a vulnerability and the
# second is a degradation, so the safe direction is to trust nothing until told.
#
# Production is nginx behind an ALB and sets 2. Each hop appends to
# X-Forwarded-For, so Strapi sees `<client>, <alb>` and has to be told how many
# to discount.
#
# A count and nothing else — Koa takes a count and a boolean, so the CIDR and
# "loopback" forms the website's TRUST_PROXY accepts are not available here.
# Anything unparseable refuses the boot.

TRUST_PROXY=
# "vite" or "static". Empty means static in production and vite elsewhere.
#
# Whether this process builds the browser's assets or merely serves what
# `react-router build` already wrote. Its own variable because serving the built
# output on a developer's machine is how a rendering fault that only appears in
# the production bundle gets reproduced — and that should not require claiming
# to be production, which would move APP_ENV and everything that comes to depend
# on it.
#
# Refuses the boot on anything else, unlike HTTP_SERVER_PORT which falls back. A
# misspelt serve mode read as its default is invisible: the override silently did
# not take.
SERVE_MODE=

# ─── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs the session cookies. Random, and at least two, comma-separated: Strapi
# signs with the first and still accepts the rest, which is what makes a
# rotation possible without logging everybody out.
APP_KEYS=development-key-one,development-key-two

# Random. Salts the hash of every API token, including the registration relay's.
API_TOKEN_SALT=development-api-token-salt

# Random. Signs admin sessions. Rotating it logs every editor out.
ADMIN_JWT_SECRET=development-admin-jwt-secret

# Random. Salts transfer tokens. Still required even with the transfer receiver
# disabled, because the admin panel mints tokens regardless.
TRANSFER_TOKEN_SALT=development-transfer-token-salt

# Random. Encrypts stored secrets, SHA-256'd to a key before use.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=development-encryption-key-0000000000000000

# Random. Signs users-permissions JWTs.
#
# Read by the plugin rather than by anything in config/, which is why it looks
# unused. It is not optional in production: with NODE_ENV set to anything but
# "development" the plugin throws at boot rather than inventing one. In
# development it generates a value and appends it to whatever ENV_PATH names.
JWT_SECRET=development-users-permissions-jwt-secret


# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "sqlite" or "postgres". Anything else throws — config/database.ts refuses to
# fall through to a default, because a misconfigured deployment quietly pointing
# at a local SQLite file is the exact failure that check exists to prevent.
#
# Empty is not an unknown value: it resolves to postgres in production and
# sqlite elsewhere. Either can be asked for anywhere — pointing a
# production-mode instance at SQLite on purpose, to reproduce a fault against a
# copy of the data, is a thing somebody needs to be able to do.
DATABASE_CLIENT=sqlite

# SQLite only. Relative to apps/cms.
DATABASE_FILENAME=env/database/data.db

# How long to wait for a connection from the pool, in milliseconds.
# DATABASE_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT=60000

# Postgres — production only. DATABASE_URL is a complete connection string and
# takes precedence; the discrete parts below are for when you would rather not
# put the password in a URL.
# DATABASE_URL=
# DATABASE_HOST=
# DATABASE_PORT=5432
# DATABASE_NAME=
# DATABASE_USERNAME=
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_SCHEMA=public

# Postgres TLS. `true` against RDS. The rest only matter with a private CA;
# leave DATABASE_SSL_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED alone unless you have one, since
# turning it off accepts any certificate at all.
# DATABASE_SSL=false
# DATABASE_SSL_CA=
# DATABASE_SSL_CAPATH=
# DATABASE_SSL_CERT=
# DATABASE_SSL_KEY=
# DATABASE_SSL_CIPHER=
# DATABASE_SSL_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true

# Connection pool bounds. The defaults suit one Strapi process; raise the
# ceiling only alongside the database's own connection limit.
# DATABASE_POOL_MIN=2
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10


# ─── Admin panel ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Whether the admin panel shows the fields that exist for the seed script rather
# than for an editor — today that is the `url` beside the file picker on an
# Image. "true" or "false"; anything else refuses the boot. Left empty, the
# environment decides, which shows them anywhere NODE_ENV is not "production".
#
# Set it to override that on one server without moving NODE_ENV, which would
# move a great deal more than one field.
ADMIN_SHOW_DEVELOPER_FIELDS=


# ─── The website ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The website's origins, comma-separated. Three config files read this through
# src/this/website-urls.ts: webtools takes the first as its absolute base, the
# admin's Entry Preview allows the whole list as origins, and the security
# middleware puts the whole list in `frame-src`.
#
# In production these must be **https** and must be the public origins a browser
# actually uses. The Entry Preview loads the site in an iframe and the browser
# matches the real origin against this list, so an http:// entry here fails a
# CSP check with nothing visibly wrong.
#
# There is no WEBSITE_URL, singular. Nothing reads one.
WEBSITE_URLS=http://localhost:9001


# ─── Registration ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The API token the website's registration relay carries. A development literal,
# like the keys and salts above: the seed plants a token with exactly this value
# so that a fresh clone has a working registration form, and the seed refuses to
# run anywhere but local SQLite.
#
# Production mints its own in the admin panel, scoped to creating a Lead and
# nothing else, and it must match CMS_API_TOKEN in the website's
# .env.production.
REGISTRATION_RELAY_TOKEN=development-registration-relay-token

# ─── Behaviour the environment only supplies defaults for ─────────────────────

# "local" or "aws-s3". Empty means aws-s3 in production and local elsewhere.
#
# Its own variable because both overrides are needed: a production-mode instance
# without S3 credentials — a staging box, a local reproduction — and a
# development instance pointed at S3, which is the only way to exercise that
# provider before production depends on it. config/middlewares.ts follows this
# rather than the environment, so the CSP always describes the provider actually
# in use.
UPLOAD_PROVIDER=

# "true" or "false". Empty means true in production and false elsewhere.
#
# Overridable in both directions: a staging site wants to prove the sitemap
# generates before production depends on it, and a production site that is not
# ready to be indexed wants it off without a deploy.
SITEMAP_ENABLED=

# ─── Uploads — if using S3 ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# config/env/production/plugins.ts switches the upload provider to S3 and reads
# all three of these, and config/env/production/middlewares.ts builds the CSP
# `img-src` and `media-src` from them. They are not optional in production:
# without them the provider misconfigures and the admin's own image previews
# break.
# AWS_REGION=
# AWS_BUCKET_NAME=
# CDN_URL=


# ─── Transfer  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Strapi's Transfer feature pulls the whole database behind one token — every
# registrant's name, email address and phone number included — and does not go
# through the content API, so none of the layers protecting the Lead route are
# in its way.
#
# Disabled is the resting state. Somebody who actually needs a transfer sets
# this for as long as they are using one and unsets it afterwards, which is a
# deliberate, visible, temporary act rather than a capability nobody remembers
# is there.
TRANSFER_REMOTE_ENABLED=


# ─── Set by PM2, not here ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NODE_ENV   "production" on both production hosts, which is what makes Strapi
#            load config/env/production/*.
# APP_ENV    Mirrors it, for code that wants the deployment rather than the
#            build mode.
# ENV_PATH   ".env.production" in production, which is what makes Strapi read
#            that file instead of this one's copy.
#
# See infra/production/cms-host/pm2/ecosystem.config.cjs.
